{"status": "ok", "message-type": "work-list", "message-version": "1.0.0", "message": {"facets": {}, "total-results": 794129, "items": [{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 10]], "date-time": "2026-07-10T17:14:42Z", "timestamp": 1783703682707, "version": "3.55.0"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "group-title": "SSRN", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Elsevier BV", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Computerised maintenance management systems record millions of building work orders, yet the field has, to our knowledge, no public scheduling benchmark and no learned dispatcher for the dispatch decision. We release an open benchmark from 3,693,867 CMMS work orders across 12 university campuses, with a real-data replay track, a calibrated generator with controllable contention, and an independent validator. Under one protocol, in a minimal model favouring simple rules, we evaluate six dispatching rules, exact and rolling CP-SAT, a genetic algorithm, and a deep reinforcement-learning dispatcher. At calibrated capacity, certified optima and due-date rules nearly coincide. Under overload, due-date rules and the learned policy tie, the most-work-remaining rule collapses by up to 90\u00d7, and rolling re-optimisation needs periodic replanning. The dispatcher reaches parity at 0.14 ms per decision but never beats tuned rules; we report this negative result in full under the released protocol. All instances, code, and results are public.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.7095162", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 10]], "date-time": "2026-07-10T16:37:22Z", "timestamp": 1783701442000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["When Does Learned Dispatching Beat Priority Rules? An Open Benchmark for Technician-Constrained Maintenance Work-Order Scheduling in Building Portfolios"], "prefix": "10.2139", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0002-4297-296X", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Ziheng", "family": "Zhang", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-3871-7199", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Tzu  Pei", "family": "Ku Chia", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "78", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 10]], "date-time": "2026-07-10T16:37:22Z", "timestamp": 1783701442000}, "score": 26.674065, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.ssrn.com/abstract=7095162"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7095162", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 1]], "date-time": "2026-02-01T02:20:19Z", "timestamp": 1769912419448, "version": "3.49.0"}, "reference-count": 96, "publisher": "Pensoft Publishers", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2024, 12, 6]], "date-time": "2024-12-06T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1733443200000}, "content-version": "vor", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://creativecommons.org/share-your-work/public-domain/cc0/"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["NB"], "abstract": "<jats:p>Understanding how some introduced plants achieve invasive status while most simply become naturalized is a fundamental question in invasion ecology. Traditional approaches comparing native and introduced plants have linked ruderal traits such as annual life history, high fecundity, and rapid growth rates to invasiveness. However, they do not explain why other introduced species bearing similar traits fail to become invasive, possibly because generic comparisons ignore local processes that drive community assembly. Herein, we contrasted native and introduced annuals in the context of local successional processes to elucidate how introduced annual bromes like cheatgrass (<jats:italic>Bromus tectorum</jats:italic>) overtake perennial grasslands in the intermountain western United States. We created disturbed plots and seeded them first with annuals representing natives, naturalized species, or invasive bromes. We then seeded plots with native perennial community dominants to examine how the different annuals influenced succession. Native annuals established transient populations that facilitated perennial establishment compared to unseeded controls, enabling the shift to perennial dominance. Naturalized annuals mirrored the natives, but invasive annuals maintained robust populations at high biomass that inhibited perennial establishment and impeded succession. Mechanistically, invasive annuals reduced soil moisture and elevated plant biomass, litter, and soil N. However, only litter abundance correlated with perennial seedling recruitment across treatments. Overall, litter showed a unimodal relationship wherein lower litter abundance associated with native and naturalized annuals appeared to facilitate perennial seedling establishment while higher litter levels generated by invasives appeared to suppress perennial establishment and inhibit succession. Additional experiments provided little support for the roles of pathogen spill-over or plant-soil feedbacks favoring the introduced bromes. The domination of perennial grasslands by annual bromes may be driven by litter buildup that allows these introduced plants to break local succession rules by acting as both early seral and climax species. Traits like litter accumulation may strongly influence invasion outcomes but are indistinguishable using trait comparisons lacking community context.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.3897/neobiota.96.129679", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2024, 12, 6]], "date-time": "2024-12-06T13:15:37Z", "timestamp": 1733490937000}, "page": "299-324", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 2, "title": ["Invaders break assembly rules to beat the natives: how cheatgrass cheats"], "prefix": "10.3897", "volume": "96", "author": [{"given": "Dean E.", "family": "Pearson", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}, {"given": "Yvette K.", "family": "Ortega", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}, {"given": "Ylva", "family": "Lekberg", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "2258", "published-online": {"date-parts": [[2024, 12, 6]]}, "reference": [{"key": "129679_B1", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.3170/2008-8-18415"}, {"key": "129679_B2", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1111/gcb.12046"}, {"key": "129679_B3", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1007/s11258-014-0307-1"}, {"key": "129679_B4", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1111/j.1365-2745.2009.01599.x"}, {"key": "129679_B5", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1371/journal.pone.0151058"}, {"key": "129679_B6", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1016/j.tree.2008.10.008"}, {"key": "129679_B7", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1046/j.1365-2745.2003.00739.x"}, {"key": "129679_B8", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1007/s10530-017-1641-8"}, {"key": "129679_B9", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1111/j.1365-2745.2010.01695.x"}, {"key": "129679_B10", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1007/978-3-319-24930-8_2"}, {"key": "129679_B11", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1038/nature02322"}, {"key": "129679_B12", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1007/BF00317337"}, {"key": "129679_B13", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1111/1365-2745.12615"}, {"key": "129679_B14", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1111/j.1472-4642.2008.00521.x"}, {"key": "129679_B15", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1007/s10530-018-1668-5"}, {"key": "129679_B16", "doi-asserted-by": "pu